            PyErr_WriteUnraisable(s);
            Py_DECREF(s);
        }
        else {
            PyErr_WriteUnraisable(Py_None);
        }
    }
};
```

I notice the warning mechanism has a complex error handling path. The function tries to create a string and write an unraisable error if the warning fails. If string creation fails, it uses `Py_None` as a fallback.

The warning behavior depends on the warning filter. With `'always'`, it triggers the warning mechanism, which might interact unexpectedly with the existing error state during array deallocation.

The key observation is how error handling and warning mechanisms interact during the array's deallocation process, potentially causing unexpected error state clearing.

